\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A positive gauge transfer with a non-Markov square root}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Take one vertex and one self-loop, #{G=SU(2)} and
 #{V=0}. The gauge action is conjugation, so the
 [physical space](fdip-0083) consists of class functions.
 Write #{\chi_n} for the irreducible character of dimension
 #{n+1}. With #{\epsilon=1/10}, choose
##{a_0=1,\qquad a_1=1/8,\qquad
 a_n=\frac{\epsilon^{2n}}{(n+1)^4}\quad(n\ge2),}
and set #{k=\sum_{n\ge0}(n+1)a_n\chi_n}.
 The bound #{|\chi_n|\le n+1} gives uniform absolute
 convergence and
##{\sum_{n\ge1}(n+1)^2a_n
 =\frac12+\sum_{n\ge2}\frac{\epsilon^{2n}}{(n+1)^2}
 <\frac12+\frac{\epsilon^4}{1-\epsilon^2}<1.}
Thus #{k} is continuous and strictly positive. All SU(2)
 characters are real and inversion symmetric, so #{k} is as well.
 Character orthogonality gives #{\int k\,\extd\mu=1}; every
 coefficient eigenvalue is positive and their dimension-weighted
 sum is finite. These are exactly the
 [transfer-kernel hypotheses](fdip-007Z).}

\p{Here #{T=C_k}, and its eigenvalues are #{a_n\le1}.
 Hence #{\lambda_0=1} and #{\Omega=1}. Its positive square
 root is convolution by
##{q(g)=\sum_{n\ge0}(n+1)\sqrt{a_n}\chi_n(g).}
Indeed, for #{n\ge2},
 #{(n+1)^2\sqrt{a_n}=\epsilon^n}, so this series converges
 uniformly absolutely. Its convolution acts by #{\sqrt{a_n}}
 on each Peter–Weyl coefficient space, identifying the operator
 with #{T^{1/2}} by the block eigenvalues in the continuum
comparison of \ref{fdip-0080}.
 The center acts on the #{n}-th irreducible by #{(-1)^n}; hence
##{q(-\bbI)=1-\sqrt2+\sum_{n\ge2}(-1)^n\epsilon^n
 =1-\sqrt2+\frac{\epsilon^2}{1+\epsilon}<0.}
For the last inequality, #{\sqrt2>7/5} and
 #{\epsilon^2/(1+\epsilon)=1/110}.}

\p{This sign gives failure on an #{L^2} set, not just at one
 point. By continuity of #{(g,h)\mapsto q(gh^{-1})}, choose
 neighborhoods #{A} of #{-\bbI}, #{B} of #{\bbI}, and #{\delta>0}
 such that #{q(gh^{-1})<-\delta} for #{g\in A,h\in B}.
 A sufficiently small ball around #{\bbI} in a bi-invariant metric
 is contained in #{B} and is conjugation invariant. The function
 #{f(h)=\max(0,1-\operatorname{dist}(h,\bbI)/\eta)} for a suitably small radius
 #{\eta>0} is a continuous nonnegative central bump supported
 in #{B}. It is positive near #{\bbI}, so #{\int f\,\extd\mu>0}
 by Haar full support from \ref{fdip-007B}. Therefore
##{(T^{1/2}f)(g)=\int q(gh^{-1})f(h)\,\extd\mu(h)
 <-\delta\int f\,\extd\mu<0\qquad(g\in A).}
The open set #{A} has positive Haar measure. Both #{f} and
 its image are class functions, so the failure occurs on the
 physical space itself.}

\p{The spectral evolution of \ref{fdip-00NA} still exists,
 and its half-step is a positive self-adjoint operator.
 A continuous-time probability interpretation additionally needs
 preservation of the function cone at every time, for example
 from a proved Markov semigroup or Dirichlet-form construction.}
